# Commit Conventions

When creating a git commit, write the message in English using this format:

```
type: short imperative summary
```

- One primary `type` per commit — pick the intent that best describes the change
- Imperative mood, lowercase after the colon, no trailing period
- Example: `feat: add email classification` — not `Added email classification.` or `feat: Add Email Classification`

## Types

| Type | Use when | Example |
|------|----------|---------|
| `feat` | New feature or capability | `feat: add email classification` |
| `fix` | Bug fix | `fix: resolve deployment issue` |
| `docs` | Documentation only | `docs: update README` |
| `style` | Formatting, CSS/UI visual, or lint — no logic change | `style: improve chat interface` |
| `refactor` | Restructure code without changing behavior | `refactor: simplify email service` |
| `test` | Add or change tests | `test: add email agent tests` |
| `chore` | Maintenance or config that is neither feature nor bug | `chore: update project dependencies` |
| `build` | Build system or dependency changes | `build: update Docker configuration` |
| `ci` | CI/CD and automation | `ci: add GitHub Actions workflow` |
| `perf` | Performance improvement | `perf: optimize email retrieval` |
| `revert` | Revert a previous commit | `revert: revert email classification` |

Create a commit only when the user asks. Follow the existing git safety protocol (no force push, no skipping hooks, no amending unless requested).
